# The front door: TLS, the body limit, the /api rewrite, the SPA fallback.
#
# This file is the contract a deployment's own ingress must reproduce if it replaces
# this container (plan 030, decision 3; deploy/README.md walks it as one paragraph):
# terminate TLS, cap request bodies on /api/*, serve the bundle with an SPA fallback,
# forward /api/* with the prefix stripped and /.well-known/oauth-* with the path
# intact, serve the two halves of browser sign-in, and set CARNET_PUBLIC_ORIGIN to
# the external origin plus /api.
#
# And since step 108: do not buffer the response body. /api/v1/chat/completions
# streams a model's answer as text/event-stream while it is still arriving, and an
# ingress that holds a body to inspect it turns every streamed completion into a
# wait for the whole answer — which plan 108 named as the thing engineers would fork
# the agent to bypass. Caddy flushes text/event-stream without being asked and sets
# no response timeout by default, so this file needs no setting for it; a
# replacement ingress does (nginx: proxy_buffering off on that path), and one with
# a response timeout under CARNET_MODEL_MAX_SECONDS ends completions early.
#
# TLS is on by default: a real CARNET_DOMAIN gets an ACME certificate (which needs
# DNS pointing here and ports 80/443 reachable); `localhost` is signed by Caddy's own
# internal CA, so a browser warns until that CA is trusted. Since step 109 a company
# whose name Let's Encrypt cannot see, or whose own CA issues its certificates, sets
# CARNET_TLS_MODE — `internal`, or `files` with the pair mounted — and the
# {$CARNET_TLS} line below is where that lands. The default this file exists to
# abolish is plain HTTP.

{
	# A config endpoint on a front door is surface nobody asked for.
	admin off
}

{$CARNET_DOMAIN}

# The certificate (step 109): empty for today's behaviour — ACME for a real name,
# Caddy's internal CA for localhost — or `tls internal`, or `tls <cert> <key>` for a
# company's own. Composed by frontdoor-entrypoint.sh from CARNET_TLS_MODE, which
# refuses a mode whose files are missing before Caddy ever reads this line.
{$CARNET_TLS}

# Client addresses are recorded here, at the terminating proxy, because the API
# deliberately reads no forwarded header — PUBLIC_ORIGIN is configuration, not a Host
# header, and nothing inside consumes X-Forwarded-*. `docker compose logs front` is
# where "who called" lives.
log

# The response headers the bundle cannot set for itself.
#
# The CSP here is THE app policy, not a supplement (plan 031, decision 2): the
# bundle's meta tag keeps only the provider-independent directives, because
# `connect-src`/`frame-src` must name the deployment's identity provider — which only
# the deployment knows — and two CSPs are enforced as their *intersection*, so a
# header could never widen a meta directive. The value is composed at container start
# by frontdoor-entrypoint.sh from CARNET_OIDC_*, the same declaration that writes
# /config.json, which is what keeps the two halves from ever disagreeing. It also
# carries `frame-ancestors` — the directive the spec ignores in a meta tag, so until
# 030 the deployed app had no clickjacking protection at all — as 'self', not 'none':
# the silent-renewal iframe's last hop is /login/callback framed by this app itself,
# and 'none' blocks it (found by a real browser; every other origin stays blocked).
#
# `Referrer-Policy` answers a hazard `routes_connections.py` names out loud — an OAuth
# code ending up "in any proxy's access log, and in the Referer".
header {
	X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
	Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
}

# The app's policy, and **not on the bundled provider's own pages** (step 121). Two
# policies on one response enforce as their INTERSECTION, and the app's is
# `form-action 'none'` — which is right for the app, whose forms are all fetch calls,
# and fatal for a provider page whose whole job is to POST a password. Stamped on
# `/idp/*` it met that page's `form-action 'self'` and produced `'none'`: registration
# and sign-in were **unsubmittable in a real browser**, with every HTTP-level check
# passing, because a CSP is enforced by a browser and by nothing else. Found by
# `e2e_browser_bundled.py` on its first run — which is the second time this project
# has paid for that sentence (plan 031 was the first).
#
# The provider sends its own, stricter policy on those pages (`localidp/edge.py`,
# `_PAGE_CSP`), so excluding them here leaves exactly one policy per response rather
# than none. Under `CARNET_IDP=external` nothing serves `/idp/*` at all and this
# matcher is inert.
@app not path /idp/*
header @app Content-Security-Policy "{$CARNET_CSP}"

# HSTS, but never on a `localhost` trial: the policy is keyed by host and ignores the
# port, so pinning `localhost` would force HTTPS on every other local development
# server on this machine — a cost paid outside this deployment entirely. A year, and
# deliberately without `includeSubDomains`: this deployment may share a domain with
# services that are not ours, and pinning them is the customer's policy call.
@public not host localhost
header @public Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"

# Everything under /api is authenticated. 12MiB is a backstop against the absurd, not
# a security boundary — the door's own per-call cap (CARNET_MCP_MAX_CALL_BYTES) is
# far lower — but "unlimited" is not an answer to how large a request may be.
handle_path /api/* {
	request_body {
		max_size 12MiB
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8000
}

# Step 083. The door's OAuth discovery documents — RFC 9728 and RFC 8414 put them at
# the origin root, not under /api — are the API's, forwarded with the path intact.
# Before the fallback for /config.json's reason below: answered by the fallback they
# would be index.html with a 200, and an MCP client would report a JSON parse error
# about a server that is plainly up.
handle /.well-known/oauth-* {
	reverse_proxy api:8000
}

# How the app finds its identity provider: a file the entrypoint writes from
# CARNET_OIDC_*, handled *before* the fallback for the same reason as /assets/*
# below — and here the fallback was not merely confusing, it was the defect: it
# answered an unconfigured /config.json with index.html and a 200, so the app's
# "response.ok" was true, the JSON parse failed into a catch written for an ordinary
# 404, and a deployment where NOBODY could sign in reported nothing anywhere. Absent
# must be a real 404.
handle /config.json {
	root * /srv
	# Revalidate every time. Nothing here sets a max-age, but a response with only an
	# ETag is *heuristically* cacheable, so a deployment that changed its provider and
	# restarted could go on serving the old one to a returning browser — a stale
	# identity provider being the one kind of staleness that ends in a failed sign-in
	# nobody can explain. A 304 costs a round trip and settles it.
	header Cache-Control "no-cache"
	file_server
}

# The bundled identity provider, or nothing (step 121). Written by
# frontdoor-entrypoint.sh from CARNET_IDP: under `bundled` this file routes /idp/* to
# the `idp` service, and under `external` it is empty. Imported rather than made
# conditional here, because this file stays static and declarative and the entrypoint
# is the one surface where logic lives — the same division the CSP and /config.json
# already follow. Before the fallback, for /config.json's reason: the provider's
# discovery document answered with index.html is a JSON parse error about a server
# that is plainly up.
import /etc/caddy/idp.caddy

# Hashed build output, and it is handled *before* the fallback on purpose: a missing
# asset must be a 404, not index.html with a 200. Sent through the fallback — which is
# what a bare `try_files` does — a half-copied or stale build answers every missing
# script with HTML, and the browser reports a MIME type error pointing nowhere near
# the cause. Found by the e2e asking for an asset that does not exist.
handle /assets/* {
	root * /srv
	file_server
}

# The bundle. The fallback is what makes a reload on /runs/abc123 land in the app
# rather than on a 404 — the same single-entry shape vite.config.ts documents.
handle {
	root * /srv
	try_files {path} /index.html
	file_server
}
